00:19:19.457 --> 00:19:20.825
So we did remove that molar.

00:19:20.825 --> 00:19:21.960
MARY: Good.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:23.462
ERIN: She did have
some associated sores

00:19:23.462 --> 00:19:24.963 align:start size:91% position:9%
on the back of her tongue
and on her throat

00:19:24.963 --> 00:19:26.631
that were sort of infection.

00:19:26.631 --> 00:19:28.233 align:start size:84% position:16%
Treated her with
a two-week antibiotic

00:19:28.233 --> 00:19:29.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
so you don't have to give her
anything, which is great.

00:19:29.667 --> 00:19:30.769
MARY: Great.

00:19:30.769 --> 00:19:32.170 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIN: She also has
pain medication on board

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:34.139
that'll last another 12 hours.

00:19:34.139 --> 00:19:37.075
She is very feral,
but she's very kind.

00:19:37.075 --> 00:19:38.510
You can release her
into the garage,

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.412 align:start position:0%
and if you can keep her in there
for another day or two

00:19:40.412 --> 00:19:42.580 align:start size:88% position:13%
just to make sure
she's eating, drinking.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:43.782
MARY: Sure.

00:19:43.782 --> 00:19:46.084
I'm sure she's just
scared and tired.

00:19:46.084 --> 00:19:49.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
She had one of the worst days
of her life yesterday,

00:19:49.187 --> 00:19:51.189 align:start size:91% position:9%
but it's also one of
the best days of her life.

00:19:51.189 --> 00:19:52.256
ERIN: Exactly.

00:19:52.256 --> 00:19:54.025 align:start size:81% position:19%
I really do believe,
left untreated,

00:19:54.025 --> 00:19:55.360
she would have deteriorated.

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:56.828 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm grateful that you
took care of her.

00:19:56.828 --> 00:19:58.430
She's grateful.

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:00.365
MARY: I'm grateful you all were
available to come help get her.

00:20:00.365 --> 00:20:02.134
ERIN: Well, it just
worked out perfect.

00:20:02.134 --> 00:20:03.835 align:start size:84% position:16%
MARY: I worried
about her last night,

00:20:03.835 --> 00:20:07.973 align:start position:0%
and it's great to have her back
and headed back home.

00:20:07.973 --> 00:20:09.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
ERIN: Please keep us posted
on how she does.

00:20:09.308 --> 00:20:10.509
MARY: I will.

00:20:10.509 --> 00:20:11.710
ERIN: Okay, thank you.
MARY: Thanks, see ya!

00:20:15.113 --> 00:20:19.551
♪ ♪

00:20:19.551 --> 00:20:21.019 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIN: We just got a call
from a client

00:20:21.019 --> 00:20:24.655 align:start size:97% position:3%
who has a Jack Russell terrier
that had two seizures.

00:20:24.655 --> 00:20:27.192
I told them immediately to load
the dog up and let's get here.

00:20:27.192 --> 00:20:28.493
The dog may walk in normal,

00:20:28.493 --> 00:20:30.462
or he could come in
completely flat.

00:20:30.462 --> 00:20:31.629
(doorbell)

00:20:31.629 --> 00:20:33.932
And I think I actually
just heard them come in.

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:45.477
ERIN: Ooh, he's having
a seizure right now.

00:20:45.477 --> 00:20:47.879 align:start size:88% position:13%
VAL: Watch your fingers.
Watch your fingers.

00:20:47.879 --> 00:20:51.149 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
ERIN: Okay. How long
have you had him?

00:20:51.149 --> 00:20:53.084 line:0%
LOGAN: It's been
a couple months.

00:20:53.084 --> 00:20:54.418
ERIN: And no seizures before?

00:20:54.418 --> 00:20:56.654 align:start size:91% position:9%
LOGAN: No. Never seen him
do this before.

00:20:56.654 --> 00:20:58.322
ERIN: This dog is
a recent adoption

00:20:58.322 --> 00:21:00.825
and its medical history
is a complete unknown.

00:21:00.825 --> 00:21:02.594
I'm going to have Val
grab anti-seizure meds

00:21:02.594 --> 00:21:03.895
while I run blood work.

00:21:03.895 --> 00:21:05.263
We've got to start
some diagnostics

00:21:05.263 --> 00:21:07.231
to see what's going on
and what's causing this.

00:21:07.231 --> 00:21:08.600
This is not a good situation.

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:09.901
Alright, Val, you got him?

00:21:09.901 --> 00:21:10.969
Okay, it's alright, buddy.

00:21:14.472 --> 00:21:15.841 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
ERIN: Val, you got him?
VAL: Okay.

00:21:15.841 --> 00:21:17.342 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
ERIN: I'm going to get blood
running first, okay.

00:21:17.342 --> 00:21:18.910 line:0%
It's alright, buddy.

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:21.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're looking for clues
as to why Rascal is seizing.

00:21:21.746 --> 00:21:23.081 align:start size:88% position:13%
The blood work
will give us information

00:21:23.081 --> 00:21:25.116 align:start size:84% position:16%
on his organ function
and electrolytes,

00:21:25.116 --> 00:21:28.920
and the other diagnostics will
hopefully point us to a cause.

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:30.789 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
Val, let's go ahead and
get an IV catheter in him.

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:32.290 line:0%
You're alright.

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:33.858 line:0%
You're alright, Rascal.

00:21:33.858 --> 00:21:35.493 line:0%
While we wait to get
the blood work results,

00:21:35.493 --> 00:21:39.931 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're gonna get a catheter in
for supportive care.

00:21:39.931 --> 00:21:40.932
Katelyn.

00:21:40.932 --> 00:21:42.033
Hold on to his butt.

00:21:42.033 --> 00:21:43.534
VAL: Rascal.

00:21:43.534 --> 00:21:45.370
ERIN: Hey, hey, hey, Rascal.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:47.371 align:start size:91% position:9%
This is an important step,
Rascal.

00:21:47.371 --> 00:21:49.608
Please be good and hold still.

00:21:49.608 --> 00:21:50.675
It's okay.

00:21:50.675 --> 00:21:52.276 align:start size:84% position:16%
LOGAN: We adopted him
two months ago.

00:21:52.276 --> 00:21:53.711 align:start size:81% position:19%
We haven't seen
any seizure activity

00:21:53.711 --> 00:21:56.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the months we've had him,
so what caused it?

00:21:56.014 --> 00:21:58.550 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
How do we make this little,
happy little dog,

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:00.051 line:0%
be a happy little dog?

00:22:00.051 --> 00:22:01.486 align:start size:81% position:19%
ERIN: Have you done
any fertilizer,

00:22:01.486 --> 00:22:04.122 align:start size:91% position:9%
anything for the crops,
anything at all like that?

00:22:04.122 --> 00:22:07.192
LOGAN: The neighbor just
did the fungicide, but...

00:22:07.192 --> 00:22:08.693
ERIN: The neighbor
did do a fungicide?

00:22:08.693 --> 00:22:11.295
LOGAN: Yeah.
ERIN: Okay.

00:22:11.295 --> 00:22:13.732 align:start size:88% position:13%
VAL: Dad's right there.
Stay here.

00:22:13.732 --> 00:22:16.267 align:start position:0%
ERIN: I'm thinking because
he's never had seizures before,

00:22:16.267 --> 00:22:17.769
and he's had three this morning,

00:22:17.769 --> 00:22:20.805
that there is potentially
some sort of ingestion.

00:22:20.805 --> 00:22:23.875 align:start size:97% position:3%
He's a farm dog,
so there are a zillion things

00:22:23.875 --> 00:22:26.712
that are around a farm that
can be poisonous to dogs.

00:22:26.712 --> 00:22:28.046
Please all work.

00:22:31.082 --> 00:22:33.051
LOGAN: I just knew
something was wrong.

00:22:33.051 --> 00:22:34.953
What's gonna happen
to this little dog?

00:22:34.953 --> 00:22:38.156 align:start size:97% position:3%
ERIN: Everything is completely
normal on his blood work.

00:22:38.156 --> 00:22:40.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
His glucose is okay,
his liver enzymes are okay,

00:22:40.792 --> 00:22:41.960
his kidneys are okay.

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:43.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
I feel like ingestion
or toxicity

00:22:43.728 --> 00:22:45.129
is a little lower down the list.

00:22:45.129 --> 00:22:47.164 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think I'd like
to just check x-rays

00:22:47.164 --> 00:22:48.933
since we've never had a seizure.

00:22:48.933 --> 00:22:51.603 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's no other great answer
that I have.

00:22:51.603 --> 00:22:57.942
(dog panting)

00:22:57.942 --> 00:22:59.077
He's always been able
to see you, right?

00:22:59.077 --> 00:23:00.311
LOGAN: Yeah.

00:23:00.311 --> 00:23:01.679
ERIN: He's never had
any issues with that?

00:23:01.679 --> 00:23:03.648
As I'm taking a look at Rascal,

00:23:03.648 --> 00:23:06.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm noticing that something
is up with his vision.

00:23:06.417 --> 00:23:08.887 align:start size:91% position:9%
That's abnormal.
He's not visual right now.

00:23:08.887 --> 00:23:10.489 align:start size:81% position:19%
There could be
something going on.

00:23:12.557 --> 00:23:16.194 align:start position:0% line:0%
ERIN: Certainly that, but it
could also be mass, brain tumor,

00:23:16.194 --> 00:23:18.162 align:start position:0%
I mean, there's a lot of things
we may not see,

00:23:18.162 --> 00:23:20.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
anything that could suddenly
cause that issue.

00:23:20.731 --> 00:23:24.502 align:start size:81% position:19%
Okay, well, let's go
get this taken.

00:23:24.502 --> 00:23:27.004
Lori, could you give Ben a call?

00:23:27.004 --> 00:23:28.540
LORI: Yeah!

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:30.108
ERIN: Thank you.

00:23:30.108 --> 00:23:31.576
Ben's our eye expert.

00:23:31.576 --> 00:23:33.344 align:start size:81% position:19%
So, while I'm taking
these x-rays,

00:23:33.344 --> 00:23:34.846
I'm gonna have him
come take a look

00:23:34.846 --> 00:23:36.948 align:start size:91% position:9%
and make sure that I'm not
missing something.

00:23:36.948 --> 00:23:42.920
♪ ♪

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:49.894
♪ ♪

00:23:49.894 --> 00:23:53.564
Logan, x-rays, really
nothing abnormal there.

00:23:53.564 --> 00:23:54.599
LOGAN: Okay.

00:23:54.599 --> 00:23:57.535 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIN: Yeah, empty tummy,
so, good things.

00:23:57.535 --> 00:23:59.537 align:start size:91% position:9%
Lots of good things.
I don't see a large tumor.

00:23:59.537 --> 00:24:02.774
We'll just get him settled down
and have Ben look at his eyes.

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:07.512
He's not painful.

00:24:07.512 --> 00:24:09.147
His x-rays look fine.

00:24:09.147 --> 00:24:12.349 align:start size:84% position:16%
His cervical vertebrae
look fine.

00:24:12.349 --> 00:24:13.818
With the vision loss is weird,

00:24:13.818 --> 00:24:15.119
that doesn't really
go with ingestion.

00:24:15.119 --> 00:24:16.655
And he's not vomiting,

00:24:16.655 --> 00:24:18.623
you know, most of these things
make them sick to their belly.

00:24:18.623 --> 00:24:19.957 align:start size:78% position:22%
He's not giving us
very many hints

00:24:19.957 --> 00:24:21.626
as to what's going on here.

00:24:21.626 --> 00:24:26.464 line:0%
♪ ♪

00:24:26.464 --> 00:24:29.901
♪ ♪

00:24:29.901 --> 00:24:32.036 align:start size:78% position:22%
BEN: Everything
looks great there.

00:24:32.036 --> 00:24:33.838
Doesn't look abnormal at all.

00:24:33.838 --> 00:24:35.039
ERIN: No hemorrhage
in the eye? Yeah.

00:24:35.039 --> 00:24:36.708
BEN: Let's say we have no vision

00:24:36.708 --> 00:24:38.409 align:start size:81% position:19%
from pressure
on the optic nerves.

00:24:38.409 --> 00:24:39.477
ERIN: Yeah.

00:24:39.477 --> 00:24:40.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
BEN: I mean,
that's very possible.

00:24:40.845 --> 00:24:42.147
ERIN: Yeah.

00:24:42.147 --> 00:24:43.481 align:start position:0%
BEN: We need some non-steroidal
anti-inflammatories

00:24:43.481 --> 00:24:45.483
to counteract that.

00:24:45.483 --> 00:24:47.318 align:start size:91% position:9%
ERIN: These meds will help
reduce the swelling

00:24:47.318 --> 00:24:49.354 align:start size:84% position:16%
that may be affecting
his vision.

00:24:49.354 --> 00:24:51.489
But the good news is, we've
ruled out everything else

00:24:51.489 --> 00:24:55.193
that we can, so this
has got to be epilepsy.

00:24:55.193 --> 00:24:57.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think we have to treat him
like an epileptic.

00:24:57.328 --> 00:24:58.896 align:start size:78% position:22%
We need to control
these seizures.

00:24:58.896 --> 00:25:00.331 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's supportive care,
and wait and see.

00:25:00.331 --> 00:25:01.332
BEN: Yeah.

00:25:01.332 --> 00:25:04.802 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIN: Okay, let's start
with that.

00:25:04.802 --> 00:25:08.139 align:start size:91% position:9%
Epilepsy is a condition
you diagnose by exclusion,

00:25:08.139 --> 00:25:10.274 align:start size:91% position:9%
and I feel very confident
that we've ruled out

00:25:10.274 --> 00:25:13.411 align:start position:0%
all these other culprits that
could be causing these seizures.

00:25:13.411 --> 00:25:15.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we're gonna start Rascal
on anti-seizure meds,

00:25:15.647 --> 00:25:17.815 align:start position:0%
and we're gonna have our intern,
Katelyn, sit with him

00:25:17.815 --> 00:25:20.818 align:start size:97% position:3%
this afternoon and monitor
for any breakthrough seizures.

00:25:20.818 --> 00:25:22.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
Katelyn, I think we put him
in that kennel

00:25:22.286 --> 00:25:24.122 line:0%
and he's gonna really get upset.

00:25:24.122 --> 00:25:27.825 line:0%
So, whatever chair
you want to sit in,

00:25:27.825 --> 00:25:29.994 align:start size:88% position:13%
you want
intern-plus-plus duties,

00:25:29.994 --> 00:25:31.996
is to sit out and
hang out with him,

00:25:31.996 --> 00:25:33.365
and you're on seizure watch.

00:25:35.199 --> 00:25:36.535 line:0%
ERIN: Yell.

00:25:38.836 --> 00:25:43.475
♪ ♪

00:25:43.475 --> 00:25:45.310 align:start position:0%
BEN: Chase and I are heading out
to Austin's farm

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:47.111
for a checkup on his calf.

00:25:47.111 --> 00:25:49.013
The hernia surgery
went really well.

00:25:49.013 --> 00:25:50.148
Hey, Austin.

00:25:50.148 --> 00:25:51.215
AUSTIN: How's it going?

00:25:51.215 --> 00:25:52.550
BEN: But we truly won't know

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:54.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
until we can get a good look
at that belly,

00:25:54.452 --> 00:25:56.054
make sure the sutures
are holding tight,

00:25:56.054 --> 00:25:59.290
and everything is where it
should be, and that's inside.

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:01.358
Ooh, she's kicking today.

00:26:01.358 --> 00:26:03.461
We got a little different calf
here today, don't we Austin?

00:26:03.461 --> 00:26:04.963 align:start position:0%
AUSTIN: A little bit,
from the last time you saw her.

00:26:04.963 --> 00:26:06.464
BEN: Feels good.

00:26:06.464 --> 00:26:08.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
Okay, I'm gonna carefully
not trying to get kicked here.

00:26:08.733 --> 00:26:11.535
AUSTIN: Put her on her side?

00:26:11.535 --> 00:26:12.938
BEN: Okay, ready?

00:26:12.938 --> 00:26:14.372
Oh. There we go.

00:26:14.372 --> 00:26:15.974 align:start size:81% position:19%
Chase, why don't you
come around here?

00:26:15.974 --> 00:26:17.808
(laughing)

00:26:17.808 --> 00:26:18.943
Help me hold legs.

00:26:18.943 --> 00:26:21.212
Good.

00:26:21.212 --> 00:26:24.048 align:start position:0%
Alright, you know, really we've
got some beautiful healing

00:26:24.048 --> 00:26:25.750
that's happened here.

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:27.752 align:start size:88% position:13%
We got just a little bit
of a area

00:26:27.752 --> 00:26:30.655 align:start position:0%
where the sutures are reacting,
and that's okay, you know.

00:26:30.655 --> 00:26:33.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
These two spots here, that's
where our big knots were.

00:26:33.725 --> 00:26:35.493 line:0%
This is just scar tissue.

00:26:35.493 --> 00:26:39.497 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
This incision went from here
all the way down to here,

00:26:39.497 --> 00:26:40.798
and it looks great.

00:26:40.798 --> 00:26:42.567 align:start size:91% position:9%
I mean, we just pulled her
out of the yard.

00:26:42.567 --> 00:26:43.735
You know, it's a rainy day.

00:26:43.735 --> 00:26:45.069 align:start size:81% position:19%
There's a little bit
of mud on it,

00:26:45.069 --> 00:26:46.805
but it's healing up
and it's closing.

00:26:46.805 --> 00:26:49.974
For what she's gone through and
with the big surgery she's had,

00:26:49.974 --> 00:26:51.676
all of her guts and intestine

00:26:51.676 --> 00:26:53.444 align:start size:81% position:19%
and all those
abdominal contents,

00:26:53.444 --> 00:26:54.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
they have no chance
of coming out anymore,

00:26:54.979 --> 00:26:56.347 align:start size:91% position:9%
and that's
the important thing here.

00:26:56.347 --> 00:26:57.983
This is a win, you know.

00:26:57.983 --> 00:26:59.016
AUSTIN: Yeah.

00:26:59.016 --> 00:27:00.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
BEN: We did
a life-saving surgery,

00:27:00.518 --> 00:27:03.187 align:start size:88% position:13%
and, um, now she can go
live out her life.

00:27:03.187 --> 00:27:04.321
AUSTIN: It wasn't
very good odds,

00:27:04.321 --> 00:27:05.723
but I'm glad we beat the odds.

00:27:05.723 --> 00:27:07.492 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, I'm glad to save her life.
Thank you.

00:27:07.492 --> 00:27:08.793
BEN: Thank you.

00:27:11.529 --> 00:27:18.502
♪ ♪

00:27:18.502 --> 00:27:19.670 line:0%
Good job, Charlie.

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:21.506 line:0%
CHARLIE: Thanks, Dad.

00:27:21.506 --> 00:27:24.242
I'm actually working.

00:27:24.242 --> 00:27:25.543
BEN: Lot of book work
when you're a vet,

00:27:25.543 --> 00:27:27.177
you know that, right?

00:27:27.177 --> 00:27:30.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
CHARLIE:
Seems like you're always
doing something over there.

00:27:30.681 --> 00:27:32.116
The Thoenes are here.

00:27:32.116 --> 00:27:34.585
BEN: I wonder what they got.

00:27:34.585 --> 00:27:35.853
The Thoene boys are kind of like

00:27:35.853 --> 00:27:37.755 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Tom Sawyers and Huck Finns
of this town.

00:27:37.755 --> 00:27:40.558
Ty, Trev, and Myles.

00:27:40.558 --> 00:27:41.926
Anytime they show up,

00:27:41.926 --> 00:27:45.563
you just know they've got
themselves into something.

00:27:45.563 --> 00:27:47.298
(laughs)

00:27:47.298 --> 00:27:49.233
Charlie, you got to see this.

00:27:49.233 --> 00:27:50.335
CHARLIE: Oh, boy.

00:27:54.305 --> 00:27:55.640 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
BEN: What do you got in there?
Let's take a look.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:57.776 line:0%
A band of local brothers
has come into the clinic,

00:27:57.776 --> 00:28:00.378 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
and what they've brought in
is wild, literally.

00:28:00.378 --> 00:28:01.478
(laughs)

00:28:01.478 --> 00:28:02.980
CHARLIE: A little baby raccoon.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:05.550
MYLES: We found the raccoon
in our shop on our farm.

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:09.354 line:0%
It's a wild animal and we didn't
really know what to do with it.

00:28:09.354 --> 00:28:10.488
BEN: They're one
of those species

00:28:10.488 --> 00:28:12.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
where we kind of think
they're nuisances

00:28:12.724 --> 00:28:15.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
or these little guys that
tear apart our garbage cans,

00:28:15.893 --> 00:28:17.528 align:start size:78% position:22%
but it's helpless,
it's an orphan.

00:28:17.528 --> 00:28:19.563
We need to take care
of this little raccoon.

00:28:19.563 --> 00:28:20.965
A raccoon is a wild animal.

00:28:20.965 --> 00:28:23.000
They can carry this parasite

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:24.902
that is not very good
for people to get.

00:28:24.902 --> 00:28:26.838
So, we want to all put on gloves

00:28:26.838 --> 00:28:29.073
and then we'll take
a look here, okay.

00:28:29.073 --> 00:28:31.642 align:start size:84% position:16%
The roundworm is shed
by raccoons,

00:28:31.642 --> 00:28:33.144
just kind of like dogs and cats,

00:28:33.144 --> 00:28:36.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
but their specific roundworm,
if it gets into a human,

00:28:36.547 --> 00:28:39.683 align:start position:0%
can migrate through your body
and go into really weird places,

00:28:39.683 --> 00:28:42.654
like your eyes or your brain.

00:28:42.654 --> 00:28:44.255
Set it right there.

00:28:44.255 --> 00:28:46.491
It's definitely something you
don't want to mess around with.

00:28:46.491 --> 00:28:49.894
It can be fatal.

00:28:49.894 --> 00:28:52.430
Oh, my goodness.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.333
Hi.

00:28:55.333 --> 00:28:56.734 align:start size:88% position:13%
Alright, let's see
what we're dealing with.

00:28:56.734 --> 00:28:58.869
A boy or a girl.

00:28:58.869 --> 00:29:00.571
Okay.

00:29:00.571 --> 00:29:02.974 align:start size:91% position:9%
Looks to be a little girl
raccoon, guys.

00:29:02.974 --> 00:29:04.275
Any names so far yet?

00:29:04.275 --> 00:29:05.276
MYLES: Ricki.

00:29:05.276 --> 00:29:06.176
BEN: Ricki.

00:29:06.176 --> 00:29:07.846
Okay, Ricki can work for a girl.

00:29:07.846 --> 00:29:08.980
Oh, my goodness.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:10.582 align:start size:84% position:16%
She's trying to nurse
anything she can.

00:29:10.582 --> 00:29:11.682
She's hungry.

00:29:11.682 --> 00:29:13.684 align:start size:84% position:16%
Let's mix up some
kitten milk replacer,

00:29:13.684 --> 00:29:16.187
and we'll definitely want
to get her dewormed, too.

00:29:16.187 --> 00:29:18.689 align:start position:0%
Every veterinarian in my opinion
needs to take care

00:29:18.689 --> 00:29:21.125
of any of these animals
that come in our door.

00:29:21.125 --> 00:29:23.594
How's that bottle
coming, Charlie?

00:29:23.594 --> 00:29:24.829
CHARLIE: Good, I'm almost done.

00:29:24.829 --> 00:29:26.130
BEN: We get caught up a lot

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.433 align:start size:97% position:3%
in trying to make our business
make money

00:29:28.433 --> 00:29:30.768
and do things that pay
our bills and feed us.

00:29:30.768 --> 00:29:32.337
Wildlife doesn't do that,

00:29:32.337 --> 00:29:34.839 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it is an obligation
in our profession.

00:29:34.839 --> 00:29:37.041
This is the dewormer.

00:29:37.041 --> 00:29:38.009
Oh...

00:29:38.009 --> 00:29:39.010
MYLES: It's not milk.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:40.979
CHARLIE: It's not milk.

00:29:40.979 --> 00:29:42.479
BEN: Alright.

00:29:42.479 --> 00:29:44.648
That was easy, wasn't it, guys?

00:29:44.648 --> 00:29:47.685 align:start size:97% position:3%
Let's see if this little baby
will nurse.

00:29:47.685 --> 00:29:50.121
Oh, she's figuring it out.

00:29:50.121 --> 00:29:51.522
CHARLIE: Wow, look
at how hungry it is.

00:29:51.522 --> 00:29:52.823 align:start size:81% position:19%
BEN: Ty, I think you
can handle this.

00:29:52.823 --> 00:29:53.891
You want to hold her?

00:29:53.891 --> 00:29:55.693
TY: Yeah.

00:29:55.693 --> 00:29:58.029
BEN: I don't think she's
giving that up for nothing.

00:29:58.029 --> 00:30:00.731
Ty, you should maybe think
about going into this field.

00:30:00.731 --> 00:30:04.669
You kind of know how to do that.

00:30:04.669 --> 00:30:07.338 align:start position:0%
I think the smartest thing to do
with a wild animal like this

00:30:07.338 --> 00:30:09.973
would be to have somebody
who's really proficient

00:30:09.973 --> 00:30:12.343
in taking care of them do that.

00:30:12.343 --> 00:30:15.880 align:start size:91% position:9%
CHARLIE: I don't know,
I kind of want to keep it.

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:18.416
BEN: You must have more of your
mom in you than what I thought,

00:30:18.416 --> 00:30:21.585
and, uh, the answer is no.

00:30:21.585 --> 00:30:23.354
CHARLIE: Dang it.

00:30:23.354 --> 00:30:25.156 align:start size:97% position:3%
BEN: The Thoene boys are going
to take this little raccoon

00:30:25.156 --> 00:30:26.824
to the animal refuge center,

00:30:26.824 --> 00:30:29.760 align:start position:0%
and they should be able to take
care of this little raccoon

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:32.396
until it's released into
the wild, which is great.

00:30:32.396 --> 00:30:35.066 align:start size:81% position:19%
Why not try to save
a life, right?

00:30:35.066 --> 00:30:36.301
It's awesome.

00:30:37.902 --> 00:30:42.907
♪ ♪

00:30:42.907 --> 00:30:47.345
♪ ♪

00:30:47.345 --> 00:30:48.713
ERIN: Hi.

00:30:48.713 --> 00:30:51.182
Alright. It's okay.

00:30:51.182 --> 00:30:52.116
I know.

00:30:52.116 --> 00:30:53.584
Your mommy's here.

00:30:53.584 --> 00:30:56.253 align:start position:0%
Our patient Rascal came into
the clinic in critical condition

00:30:56.253 --> 00:30:58.055
after a string of seizures.

00:30:58.055 --> 00:31:00.458 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
After 24 hours of observation
and treatment,

00:31:00.458 --> 00:31:01.726
we're sending him home.

00:31:01.726 --> 00:31:03.828 align:start size:84% position:16%
But he's not
out of the woods yet.

00:31:05.629 --> 00:31:08.132 align:start size:81% position:19%
STACY: Hey, boo-boo.
Hi, buddy.

00:31:08.132 --> 00:31:09.834 line:0%
(kiss)

00:31:09.834 --> 00:31:12.136 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
ERIN: There's been no more
seizures since he's been here.

00:31:12.136 --> 00:31:14.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
All the blood work came back
completely normal.

00:31:14.472 --> 00:31:17.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think he's
a little more visual.

00:31:17.375 --> 00:31:19.210 align:start size:81% position:19%
Oh, he's definitely,
that's better.

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:20.444
Are you yawning?

00:31:20.444 --> 00:31:22.980 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's really no other
reason we can find.

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:26.717
You know, that leaves
basically epilepsy.

00:31:26.717 --> 00:31:28.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
I want you to keep him
on epileptic meds

00:31:28.219 --> 00:31:30.154
to treat his seizures.

00:31:30.154 --> 00:31:32.223 align:start size:88% position:13%
A brain tumor is sort of
worse-case scenario.

00:31:32.223 --> 00:31:34.124
So, we would treat
with epileptic drugs

00:31:34.124 --> 00:31:35.793
and certainly if that
did not resolve it,

00:31:35.793 --> 00:31:38.696
then you know, have other
conversations, you know...

00:31:38.696 --> 00:31:39.697
STACY: Stay on top of it.

00:31:39.697 --> 00:31:40.731
ERIN: Stay on top of that.

00:31:40.731 --> 00:31:42.166
I'm hopeful,

00:31:42.166 --> 00:31:44.068 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I definitely want to
check up on him in a few weeks

00:31:44.068 --> 00:31:46.136 align:start size:88% position:13%
to see how the treatment
is working.

00:31:46.136 --> 00:31:48.706
STACY: He's definitely loved.

00:31:48.706 --> 00:31:50.742 align:start position:0%
So, Logan has a birthday picture
to show you.

00:31:50.742 --> 00:31:52.343 align:start size:78% position:22%
ERIN: Oh, when was
your birthday?

00:31:52.343 --> 00:31:54.178
STACY: Not ours, his.

00:31:54.178 --> 00:31:58.316
ERIN: Oh, look at him in his
sumo outfit for his birthday.

00:31:58.316 --> 00:32:00.217
(laughing)

00:32:00.217 --> 00:32:01.685
Well, call me if you need me.

00:32:01.685 --> 00:32:03.121 align:start size:78% position:22%
STACY: Okay.
ERIN: Bye, buddy.

00:32:03.121 --> 00:32:04.121
Okay, alright.

00:32:04.121 --> 00:32:05.823
STACY: Thank you.
ERIN: Thank you.

00:32:05.823 --> 00:32:07.525
STACY: Okay, ready?

00:32:08.792 --> 00:32:15.165
♪ ♪

00:32:15.165 --> 00:32:17.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
ERIN: Hi, how are you guys?
OLIVIA: Good.

00:32:17.168 --> 00:32:18.269
ERIN: Oh, my goodness.

00:32:18.269 --> 00:32:20.772 align:start size:88% position:13%
Those are the prettiest
bunnies ever.

00:32:20.772 --> 00:32:22.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
Um, you guys just want
to go into two?

00:32:22.173 --> 00:32:23.608
OLIVIA: Okay.

00:32:23.608 --> 00:32:25.909 align:start size:97% position:3%
ERIN: Olivia and her mom
are here with a ton of bunnies

00:32:25.909 --> 00:32:28.346 align:start size:81% position:19%
that she raised
for her 4-H project.

00:32:28.346 --> 00:32:30.614 align:start position:0%
BEN: So, this is a health paper
for your fair, right?

00:32:30.614 --> 00:32:33.651
OLIVIA: Yes. I've been a 4-H
member for about four years.

00:32:33.651 --> 00:32:35.420 align:start size:88% position:13%
I brought my bunnies
to the clinic here today

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:37.354 align:start size:91% position:9%
to get their normal health
checkup for the fair.

00:32:37.354 --> 00:32:39.490 line:0%
Otherwise they can't be
a member of the fair,

00:32:39.490 --> 00:32:41.459 line:0%
and that's what I do.

00:32:41.459 --> 00:32:42.994
ERIN: It's bunny time!

00:32:42.994 --> 00:32:45.529 align:start size:94% position:6%
4-H programs help teach kids
about leadership

00:32:45.529 --> 00:32:47.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
and commitment
to their communities.

00:32:47.431 --> 00:32:49.533 align:start size:84% position:16%
OLIVIA: This is Jack,
a Holland lop.

00:32:49.533 --> 00:32:50.601
BEN: Because of his lop ears.

00:32:50.601 --> 00:32:52.036
OLIVIA: Yes.

00:32:52.036 --> 00:32:53.804
ERIN: We're just checking to
make sure none of these animals

00:32:53.804 --> 00:32:55.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
have any signs
of infectious disease.

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:57.007
Sounds great.

00:32:57.007 --> 00:32:59.544 align:start size:97% position:3%
We don't want anybody bringing
something to the fair

00:32:59.544 --> 00:33:00.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they're going to share
with their neighbor.

00:33:00.979 --> 00:33:03.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
BEN: What breed is this one?
OLIVIA: A lion head.

00:33:03.347 --> 00:33:05.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
ERIN: Cutest thing I've ever
seen in my life.

00:33:05.349 --> 00:33:07.251
Oh, my gosh, honey.

00:33:07.251 --> 00:33:09.787
We have to get one
that's house-trained.

00:33:09.787 --> 00:33:11.388 align:start size:88% position:13%
BEN: That's great.
ERIN: Lungs sound great.

00:33:11.388 --> 00:33:13.057
The lion head is
a breed of rabbit

00:33:13.057 --> 00:33:15.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
that has a long mane of fur
around its head.

00:33:15.226 --> 00:33:17.928 align:start size:88% position:13%
Their origin is unknown,
but who cares,

00:33:17.928 --> 00:33:20.698 align:start size:91% position:9%
they are the cutest things
on the planet.

00:33:20.698 --> 00:33:22.033
BEN: Just a tiny
little bunny here.

00:33:22.033 --> 00:33:23.634
Nose looks good.

00:33:23.634 --> 00:33:24.702
Look, he matches my shirt.

00:33:24.702 --> 00:33:25.703
ERIN: I know.

00:33:25.703 --> 00:33:26.971
Honey, there's more bunnies.

00:33:26.971 --> 00:33:27.972
BEN: I'm good.

00:33:27.972 --> 00:33:31.109
(laughter)

00:33:31.109 --> 00:33:33.611 align:start position:0%
OLIVIA: This is a Charlie
because he has a half mustache.

00:33:33.611 --> 00:33:34.812
ERIN: Are you a Charlie?

00:33:34.812 --> 00:33:36.947 align:start size:88% position:13%
The half mustache
comes from a broken gene

00:33:36.947 --> 00:33:38.816
that's inherited
from each parent,

00:33:38.816 --> 00:33:41.252
making these rabbits look
like Charlie Chaplain.

00:33:41.252 --> 00:33:43.220
OLIVIA: Oh, that's Willie.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:45.489
BEN: Good nose.
Eyes look clear.

00:33:45.489 --> 00:33:48.259 align:start size:91% position:9%
OLIVIA: This is Buttercup.
She's a red-eyed white.

00:33:48.259 --> 00:33:49.793
BEN: How's that butt look?

00:33:49.793 --> 00:33:51.261
ERIN: That butt looks great.

00:33:51.261 --> 00:33:54.599
BEN: They've all got their
normal, hard rabbit poops?

00:33:54.599 --> 00:33:55.599
Good catch, Olivia.

00:33:55.599 --> 00:33:57.235
ERIN: Good catch. Sorry.

00:33:57.235 --> 00:33:58.301
OLIVIA: You're okay.

00:33:58.301 --> 00:33:59.470
ERIN: I am totally impressed.

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:00.805
You really know your animals

00:34:00.805 --> 00:34:03.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they are clean,
they're well taken care of.

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:04.508
OLIVIA: I love raising them

00:34:04.508 --> 00:34:07.310
'cause it kinda gives me
like a feeling of peace.

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:08.813
I spend so much time with them,

00:34:08.813 --> 00:34:11.815 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's just kind of like
my favorite thing to do now.

00:34:11.815 --> 00:34:13.150
ERIN: Good luck, my dear.
OLIVIA: Nice meeting you.

00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:14.585
ERIN: Totally. Same here,

00:34:14.585 --> 00:34:17.622
and thank you for bringing them
down today and sharing them.

00:34:19.189 --> 00:34:25.162
♪ ♪

00:34:25.162 --> 00:34:27.397
BEN: We got a late call
from a dairy farmer.

00:34:27.397 --> 00:34:29.834 align:start size:97% position:3%
One of his milk cows has been
sick for a day or two

00:34:29.834 --> 00:34:31.235
and hasn't improved.

00:34:31.235 --> 00:34:33.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
He's starting to get worried,
and rightfully so.

00:34:33.938 --> 00:34:37.374
When a cow doesn't eat, it could
mean a number of serious issues.

00:34:37.374 --> 00:34:39.376
So, Erin and I are heading
to the farm with the boys

00:34:39.376 --> 00:34:41.846
to check her out.

00:34:41.846 --> 00:34:43.114
ERIN: Got it, Chase?

00:34:43.114 --> 00:34:44.515
Oh, gosh.

00:34:44.515 --> 00:34:45.549
Hey, Mark!

00:34:45.549 --> 00:34:46.717
BEN: Quite a storm we're having.

00:34:46.717 --> 00:34:48.786
MARK: Yeah, yeah.

00:34:48.786 --> 00:34:50.020 line:0%
BEN: Can you lead us
to where she's at?

00:34:50.020 --> 00:34:51.021 line:0%
MARK: Yep.

00:34:51.021 --> 00:34:57.127
♪ ♪

00:34:57.127 --> 00:34:59.530
BEN: One thing I just noticed
is she's kind of straining.

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:00.964
MARK: Okay.

00:35:00.964 --> 00:35:03.667 align:start size:94% position:6%
BEN: You know, not much
fecal material's coming out.

00:35:03.667 --> 00:35:06.937
So, that means something
may be twisted internally.

00:35:06.937 --> 00:35:09.273 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'll take a quick listen here
and see what we got.

00:35:12.443 --> 00:35:17.147
(flicking)

00:35:17.147 --> 00:35:19.349
Chase, can I show you this?

00:35:19.349 --> 00:35:22.487
I just want you to listen to it.

00:35:22.487 --> 00:35:27.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm going to ping real hard
against her abdomen.

00:35:27.358 --> 00:35:28.693 line:0%
What's it sound like?

00:35:28.693 --> 00:35:30.060 line:0%
CHASE: Like a basketball.

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:32.196
BEN: It's full of air.

00:35:32.196 --> 00:35:34.898
So, it sounds like
a displaced abomasum.

00:35:34.898 --> 00:35:38.536 align:start size:97% position:3%
A displaced abomasum,
or more commonly called a DA,

00:35:38.536 --> 00:35:43.541
is the fourth stomach of a cow
that is shifted out of place.

00:35:43.541 --> 00:35:46.710
It always occurs from them
not eating for a day or two,

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:49.046 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then gas building up
in the stomach

00:35:49.046 --> 00:35:52.583 align:start size:94% position:6%
where there's no food
holding it down into place.

00:35:52.583 --> 00:35:54.585
Eventually they do die.

00:35:54.585 --> 00:35:57.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
The left DA,
which is what she has,

00:35:57.320 --> 00:35:58.889
is fixable with a roll, okay?

00:35:58.889 --> 00:36:00.090
MARK: Okay.

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:02.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
BEN: You basically roll them
on their back,

00:36:02.092 --> 00:36:04.795
get the abomasum
right into place.

00:36:04.795 --> 00:36:07.297 align:start position:0%
So, we can fix her without going
into her abdomen

00:36:07.297 --> 00:36:08.799
and actually having surgery...

00:36:08.799 --> 00:36:09.934
MARK: Okay.

00:36:09.934 --> 00:36:11.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
BEN: ...which is
a good thing for her.

00:36:11.235 --> 00:36:13.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
Especially, uh,
on a night like this.

00:36:13.304 --> 00:36:15.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm glad I brought everybody
along in the family

00:36:15.672 --> 00:36:18.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
because now we have
lots of hands on deck,

00:36:18.175 --> 00:36:19.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
because we need to go
cow tipping.

00:36:19.744 --> 00:36:21.579
MARK: Okay.
BEN: Okay.

00:36:21.579 --> 00:36:23.113
(laughter)

00:36:23.113 --> 00:36:24.414
We'll lightly sedate her

00:36:24.414 --> 00:36:26.416 align:start size:88% position:13%
so she doesn't fight us
during the roll.

00:36:26.416 --> 00:36:28.119
Okay, and I'm going to give it.

00:36:28.119 --> 00:36:30.187 align:start size:91% position:9%
She's going to start going
to sleep pretty quick.

00:36:30.187 --> 00:36:34.257
So, go ahead and let her loose.

00:36:34.257 --> 00:36:35.926
We're going to pull her down.

00:36:35.926 --> 00:36:39.096
She has to roll the correct way.

00:36:39.096 --> 00:36:41.198
Right side, legs over.

00:36:41.198 --> 00:36:43.200 align:start size:88% position:13%
So that way the stomach
comes up to the top

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:44.701
in the right direction.

00:36:44.701 --> 00:36:48.505
As far as difficulty, this is
probably a nine out of ten.

00:36:48.505 --> 00:36:49.873
I know a lot of veterinarians

00:36:49.873 --> 00:36:51.208 align:start size:84% position:16%
that don't like to do
this procedure

00:36:51.208 --> 00:36:52.876
because it takes practice,

00:36:52.876 --> 00:36:55.812
and luckily, I had a father
who taught me how to do it.

00:36:55.812 --> 00:36:57.381 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is not something
I learned in vet school,

00:36:57.381 --> 00:36:58.382
I promise you that.

00:36:58.382 --> 00:37:00.284 align:start size:81% position:19%
I learned it
from John Schroeder.

00:37:00.284 --> 00:37:01.685
Okay, here we go.
I'm gonna pull.

00:37:01.685 --> 00:37:05.055
Yeah, she's really bracing.

00:37:05.055 --> 00:37:06.157
CHARLIE: Come on, girl.

00:37:10.460 --> 00:37:11.461 line:0%
BEN: We're gonna pull her down.

00:37:11.461 --> 00:37:13.530 line:0%
She has to roll the correct way.

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:15.766 line:0%
Okay, here we go.
I'm going to pull.

00:37:15.766 --> 00:37:18.235
This dairy cow has
a displaced abomasum.

00:37:18.235 --> 00:37:20.804
That's basically
a twisted stomach.

00:37:20.804 --> 00:37:22.439
She's really bracing.

00:37:22.439 --> 00:37:23.707
We're rolling her on her back

00:37:23.707 --> 00:37:25.709
to get this stomach
back into place,

00:37:25.709 --> 00:37:31.048
but this 1,500-pound cow
has got ideas of her own.

00:37:31.048 --> 00:37:32.649
CHARLIE: Come on, girl.

00:37:32.649 --> 00:37:35.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
ERIN: Oh, she's going.
There we go.

00:37:35.219 --> 00:37:36.219
BEN: Yes.

00:37:36.219 --> 00:37:37.221 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIN: Oh, roll your feet
that way.

00:37:37.221 --> 00:37:39.823 align:start size:81% position:19%
BEN: Okay, hold this
tight, Mark.

00:37:39.823 --> 00:37:42.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're going to
push her over, ready?

00:37:42.993 --> 00:37:45.763
Good, just like that.

00:37:45.763 --> 00:37:47.798 align:start size:81% position:19%
BEN: 1,500 to
2,000-pound animal,

00:37:47.798 --> 00:37:50.567 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
they aren't the best breathers
when they're on their feet,

00:37:50.567 --> 00:37:52.135 line:0%
let alone on their back.

00:37:52.135 --> 00:37:53.737 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, you have to do
this procedure

00:37:53.737 --> 00:37:55.472
quickly and efficiently.

00:37:55.472 --> 00:37:58.008 line:0%
It's dangerous for them.

00:37:58.008 --> 00:38:02.746 align:start position:0%
Now, go over to that other
next post and give it one wrap.

00:38:02.746 --> 00:38:05.349 align:start size:91% position:9%
I'm going to pull
and you're going to pull.

00:38:05.349 --> 00:38:07.318
I'm going to pull,
you're going to pull.

00:38:07.318 --> 00:38:10.921
Pull. One more time.

00:38:10.921 --> 00:38:12.089
One more time.

00:38:16.059 --> 00:38:19.997
Alright, that abomasum
should be back in place.

00:38:19.997 --> 00:38:24.734
So, we're going to give her
a lot of sugar right now.

00:38:24.734 --> 00:38:26.336
Alright, I can still see a vein.

00:38:26.336 --> 00:38:28.339
That's good.

00:38:28.339 --> 00:38:30.507
(cow bellowing)

00:38:30.507 --> 00:38:32.375
So, we want her to get
back on her feet soon,

00:38:32.375 --> 00:38:36.747 align:start size:81% position:19%
and the sugar should
really do that.

00:38:36.747 --> 00:38:38.882
All done with the fluids.

00:38:38.882 --> 00:38:40.117
ERIN: She's waking up, honey.

00:38:40.117 --> 00:38:41.552
BEN: Oh, yeah, she's
about ready, isn't she?

00:38:41.552 --> 00:38:42.953
ERIN: Time to wakey, wakey.
CHARLIE: Sugar's hitting.

00:38:42.953 --> 00:38:45.623 align:start size:81% position:19%
BEN: Come on, girl.
There you go.

00:38:45.623 --> 00:38:46.891
Get up, girl.

00:38:46.891 --> 00:38:48.558 align:start size:88% position:13%
CHARLIE: You know
what your back feet are.

00:38:48.558 --> 00:38:51.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
BEN: Now, the next thing is,
she needs help herself.

00:38:51.362 --> 00:38:53.898 align:start size:91% position:9%
She needs to go right back
to eating and drinking

00:38:53.898 --> 00:38:56.467 align:start size:88% position:13%
and put a lot of weight
in that abomasum,

00:38:56.467 --> 00:38:58.402 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that will hold it
into place.

00:38:58.402 --> 00:39:01.605 align:start size:91% position:9%
Alright, I love that she's
eating and drinking.

00:39:01.605 --> 00:39:03.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIN: Well, I'd say
that that's a good sign.

00:39:03.807 --> 00:39:04.842 align:start size:81% position:19%
BEN: I'm glad
we came out tonight.

00:39:04.842 --> 00:39:06.143
I know it's wet and cold,

00:39:06.143 --> 00:39:07.811 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
but morning could have been
worse off.

00:39:07.811 --> 00:39:08.813 line:0%
MARK: Yeah, thanks
for coming tonight.

00:39:08.813 --> 00:39:09.747 line:0%
BEN: You bet.

00:39:13.183 --> 00:39:16.887
♪ ♪

00:39:16.887 --> 00:39:21.392
♪ ♪

00:39:21.392 --> 00:39:23.060 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
ERIN: So we're on our way
to go see Rascal,

00:39:23.060 --> 00:39:27.131
who is the little doggy that
we diagnosed with epilepsy,

00:39:27.131 --> 00:39:29.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
and just check in and
see how his meds are going.

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:33.069 align:start position:0%
Making sure that he's responding
and that he's comfortable,

00:39:33.069 --> 00:39:34.504 align:start size:81% position:19%
and that mom and dad
are comfortable

00:39:34.504 --> 00:39:38.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
with all the recommendations
that we've given.

00:39:38.575 --> 00:39:41.144
It is windy.

00:39:41.144 --> 00:39:42.279
(knocks)

00:39:46.016 --> 00:39:48.084 align:start size:72% position:28%
ERIN: Hi!
STACY: Hello!

00:39:48.084 --> 00:39:50.421
ERIN: How are you?

00:39:50.421 --> 00:39:52.155 line:0%
Man, he looks good!

00:39:52.155 --> 00:39:53.991 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
STACY: I know!
ERIN: How's he doing?

00:39:53.991 --> 00:39:55.158
STACY: Good! So far.

00:39:55.158 --> 00:39:57.728
ERIN: So far, so good?
Eating, drinking? Fine?

00:39:57.728 --> 00:39:58.728
STACY: Perfect.

00:39:58.728 --> 00:39:59.696
ERIN: And how about his vision?

00:39:59.696 --> 00:40:01.064
STACY: Um, so far, so good.

00:40:01.064 --> 00:40:03.433
ERIN: And is he playing
and being a happy dog?

00:40:03.433 --> 00:40:05.269 align:start size:91% position:9%
LOGAN: He's getting to be,
more and more.

00:40:05.269 --> 00:40:09.406 align:start size:97% position:3%
STACY: More social. He doesn't
chase the cats as much.

00:40:09.406 --> 00:40:11.342 align:start size:84% position:16%
ERIN: They did tip me
extra for that.

00:40:11.342 --> 00:40:14.044
STACY: Perfect!

00:40:14.044 --> 00:40:15.078 align:start size:81% position:19%
ERIN: You know,
it's really positive

00:40:15.078 --> 00:40:16.246 align:start size:78% position:22%
we haven't seen
any more seizures.

00:40:16.246 --> 00:40:17.848
STACY: Yes, absolutely.

00:40:17.848 --> 00:40:20.183 align:start size:97% position:3%
ERIN: That is something
I'll never forget watching him

00:40:20.183 --> 00:40:22.853 align:start position:0%
go through, and hopefully we
don't ever have to see it again.

00:40:22.853 --> 00:40:25.556 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, I am so glad
I got to swing by today.

00:40:25.556 --> 00:40:28.459
I mean, he's just a different
dog, he's alert, he's bright.

00:40:28.459 --> 00:40:30.527
And he just looks,
he just looks happy.

00:40:30.527 --> 00:40:32.729 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know my number,
you can call me anytime.

00:40:32.729 --> 00:40:34.097 align:start size:78% position:22%
STACY: Thank you!
ERIN: Yes.

00:40:34.097 --> 00:40:36.099
ERIN: Bye, Rascal.

00:40:36.099 --> 00:40:41.972
♪ ♪

00:40:41.972 --> 00:40:45.075 align:start size:88% position:13%
Alright, Chase, I think
this is the day.

00:40:45.075 --> 00:40:46.076 line:0%
Hey, Kerry.

00:40:46.076 --> 00:40:47.144 line:0%
KERRY: Hi, guys!

00:40:47.144 --> 00:40:48.712 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
ERIN: We are in
Yankton, South Dakota,

00:40:48.712 --> 00:40:51.214 align:start position:0%
at the Heartland Humane Society
looking for some dogs

00:40:51.214 --> 00:40:53.650
that are available for adoption.

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:56.286
You have a little
whippet-type dog.

00:40:56.286 --> 00:40:57.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
KERRY: It was adopted
this morning.

00:40:57.587 --> 00:40:59.924
ERIN: Oh, no! Well, darn it.

00:40:59.924 --> 00:41:02.625
KERRY: I got black cats and
ten other dogs back there.

00:41:02.625 --> 00:41:03.727
ERIN: What do you got for dogs?

00:41:03.727 --> 00:41:05.229
KERRY: Yeah, let's go look.

00:41:05.229 --> 00:41:08.499
(barking)

00:41:08.499 --> 00:41:11.702 align:start size:88% position:13%
ERIN: Alright, so who do
you have in here?

00:41:11.702 --> 00:41:13.837 align:start position:0%
KERRY: We've got, Nellie's new,
Great Pyrenees.

00:41:13.837 --> 00:41:14.838
ERIN: Hi, Nellie.

00:41:14.838 --> 00:41:16.573
KERRY: Really sweet, sweet girl.

00:41:16.573 --> 00:41:17.741
ERIN: Oh, she is sweet.

00:41:17.741 --> 00:41:19.176
She's a little bit big.

00:41:19.176 --> 00:41:21.912 align:start size:91% position:9%
KERRY: Little big for you,
absolutely.

00:41:21.912 --> 00:41:23.814 align:start size:91% position:9%
Both Rigs and Winnie
came in in the last week.

00:41:23.814 --> 00:41:25.048
ERIN: Hi, guys.

00:41:25.048 --> 00:41:26.416
KERRY: She's probably
still growing, though.

00:41:26.416 --> 00:41:28.018
She's only seven months old.

00:41:28.018 --> 00:41:31.054
ERIN: I'm not sure I could carry
you up the stairs when you age.

00:41:31.054 --> 00:41:33.390 align:start size:88% position:13%
'Cause how old are you?
Two years.

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:34.992
Do you have any little dogs?

00:41:34.992 --> 00:41:37.094 align:start size:91% position:9%
KERRY: Yeah, the only two
I have are paired.

00:41:37.094 --> 00:41:38.828
They've got to go together.

00:41:38.828 --> 00:41:40.664 align:start size:81% position:19%
ERIN: Well, this is
the right size.

00:41:40.664 --> 00:41:42.733 align:start size:81% position:19%
KERRY: This is Happy
and Skittles.

00:41:42.733 --> 00:41:43.767
ERIN: Hi, guys.

00:41:43.767 --> 00:41:45.369
KERRY: Their owner
just got too old.

00:41:45.369 --> 00:41:48.672
So, they're looking for a fresh
family to finish life out with.

00:41:48.672 --> 00:41:51.208
They definitely are
happy to see you.

00:41:51.208 --> 00:41:52.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
ERIN: There's just something
there I really like

00:41:52.509 --> 00:41:53.677
about these two little dogs.

00:41:53.677 --> 00:41:55.546 align:start size:81% position:19%
They're kind of like
"The Odd Couple."

00:41:55.546 --> 00:41:57.281 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
ERIN: Do you like them?
CHASE: Yeah.

00:41:57.281 --> 00:42:02.085 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
ERIN: They're pretty cute.
But it's two. It's two.

00:42:02.085 --> 00:42:04.421
Oh, my gosh, I'm going
to end up a single woman

00:42:04.421 --> 00:42:05.822 align:start position:0%
with two kids I have to care of
and two dogs.

00:42:05.822 --> 00:42:06.990
KERRY: You want
to get them out?

00:42:06.990 --> 00:42:09.493 align:start size:84% position:16%
ERIN: Yeah. Let's see.
Let's get them out.

00:42:09.493 --> 00:42:10.627
Hi, guys.

00:42:10.627 --> 00:42:14.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
Hi, oh, well,
aren't you convincing?

00:42:14.131 --> 00:42:15.865
Chase and I love
these little dogs.

00:42:15.865 --> 00:42:17.968
Happy is awesome.

00:42:17.968 --> 00:42:20.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
Skittles is so snuggly
and cuddly.

00:42:20.537 --> 00:42:21.871
But it's still two dogs.

00:42:21.871 --> 00:42:24.274 align:start size:91% position:9%
So, this isn't a decision
to take lightly.

00:42:24.274 --> 00:42:27.878
Oh, he's snuggling you.

00:42:27.878 --> 00:42:31.047
(mooing)

00:42:31.047 --> 00:42:37.153
♪ ♪

00:42:37.153 --> 00:42:38.355
(horn honks)

00:42:38.355 --> 00:42:39.355
CHARLIE: It's Mom.

00:42:39.355 --> 00:42:40.357
BEN: Let's go see what she got.

00:42:40.357 --> 00:42:42.058
ERIN: Here, you stay here.

00:42:42.058 --> 00:42:43.460
BEN: Hey, hon, how'd it go?

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:47.330
ERIN: Well, we got a dog.

00:42:47.330 --> 00:42:49.533 align:start size:84% position:16%
BEN: Aw, he's awesome,
look at you, buddy.

00:42:49.533 --> 00:42:50.801
ERIN: This is Skittles.

00:42:50.801 --> 00:42:52.202 align:start size:88% position:13%
CHARLIE: Hey, Skittles!
BEN: Skittles?

00:42:52.202 --> 00:42:53.537
ERIN: And Happy.

00:42:53.537 --> 00:42:55.139
BEN: What? Two dogs?

00:42:55.139 --> 00:42:57.541
ERIN: Two dogs!

00:42:57.541 --> 00:42:59.076
BEN: Man, I had no idea.

00:42:59.076 --> 00:43:01.545 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was thinking she might
just go and look.

00:43:01.545 --> 00:43:03.480 align:start size:88% position:13%
We talked about one dog,
but two dogs?

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:04.547
What happened here?

00:43:04.547 --> 00:43:07.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
ERIN: These guys were
a bonded pair,

00:43:07.551 --> 00:43:09.653
and they are 8 and 12.

00:43:09.653 --> 00:43:10.721
BEN: Oh, wow, I like it.

00:43:10.721 --> 00:43:12.055 align:start size:81% position:19%
So, they're probably
housebroken.

00:43:12.055 --> 00:43:13.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
ERIN: They're supposed
to be housebroken.

00:43:13.990 --> 00:43:16.326 align:start size:81% position:19%
They've been around
kitties, kids,

00:43:16.326 --> 00:43:17.927
and you had to take them both.

00:43:17.927 --> 00:43:20.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
We went up there to get Bud,
and he had been adopted,

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:23.267
so Kerry just kind of
showed us what she had,

00:43:23.267 --> 00:43:24.268
